<% content_for :title, "#{@node.name} — Tinkerex" %>
<% content_for :container_class, "container-wide" %>
<%# THE BREADCRUMB IS "BACK TO FOLDER" (structural redesign §5.3, slice 3): the folder chain
    FilesController#show walks with the same folder_crumbs #index uses. The link to THIS FILE'S
    folder carries `selected:` for it, so the Library reopens with the file selected and its
    inspector open. A file at the library root has no folder crumb, so "Files" is its folder and
    carries the selection instead. The page head's Back keeps its referer contract
    (ApplicationHelper#back_target_path) and is not this link. %>
<% content_for :crumbs do %>
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="File path">
    <%= link_to "Files", files_path(selected: (@crumbs.empty? ? @node.node_id : nil)) %>
    <% @crumbs.each_with_index do |crumb, i| %>
      <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <%= link_to crumb.name, files_path(folder: crumb.node_id,
                                         selected: (i == @crumbs.length - 1 ? @node.node_id : nil)) %>
    <% end %>
    <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
    <span aria-current="page"><%= @node.name %></span>
  </nav>
<% end %>

<%#
  Reads @info (FileLayer::FileInfo) rather than a Version: Version is chunk-mode only, and on a
  share/s3 library this page used to report the truth about the wrong object -- Size "—" and
  "No content yet." for a file sitting on the user's NAS.

  `capabilities` is the controller's helper_method. It is called, not ivar-dereferenced with &.,
  because a nil ivar is silently falsy in .erb and would make a gated feature disappear with
  nothing red anywhere; `capabilities.versioned?` raises instead. Loud beats convenient here.

  content_token, not size, is the "has content" test: it is nil exactly when there is nothing to
  show -- no version yet in chunk mode, a directory in any mode -- whereas a size of 0 is a real
  empty file the user created.

  THE PREVIEW ITSELF IS EIGHT PARTIALS UNDER files/viewers/ (stage 4, S4-5), and this page is one
  of TWO that render them: Public::FilesController#view renders the same eight for a visitor,
  pointed at public URLs. Each takes every URL as a strict local for that reason, so nothing below
  the branch chain knows which page it is on. This page reaches them through files/_viewer
  (2026-10-21), the maker's chain, which the model page's pane renders too.
%>

<%# The saved addon item this file came out of, or nil. Read ONCE into a local because two places
    below need it — the button and the meta row — and re-digging would invite the two to fall out of
    step. An absent key is the ordinary case: most files were not made by an addon. %>
<% addon_origin = @addon_origins[@node.node_id] %>

<div class="page-head">
  <div class="page-title">
    <h1><%= @node.name %></h1>
    <% ext = File.extname(@node.name).delete(".").upcase %>
    <% if ext.present? %><span class="badge file-badge"><%= ext %></span><% end %>
  </div>
  <div class="page-actions">
    <%# Reopens the generator on the saved item that produced this file, with its parameters
        already loaded (the ?state= deep link Tools::AddonsController resolves into ctx.launch).
        The label names the addon and is built once, in AddonOrigins. %>
    <% if addon_origin %>
      <%= link_to addon_origin[:label], addon_origin[:url], class: "btn" %>
    <% end %>
    <%= link_to "Download", blob_file_path(@node.node_id, download: 1), class: "btn" %>
    <%= button_to "Delete", file_path(@node.node_id), method: :delete,
          form: { data: { turbo_confirm: "Delete #{@node.name}?" } }, class: "btn danger" %>
    <%# Back to WHERE THE USER CAME FROM (the folder, the filter, the project panel), not to the
        library root — see ApplicationHelper#back_target_path. files_path stays as the fallback for
        a direct visit, which is what this link always did. %>
    <%= link_to "Back", back_target_path(default: files_path), class: "btn ghost" %>
  </div>
</div>

<%= render "projects/move_conflict" %>

<%# THE WORKSPACE (structural redesign §5.3, slice 3): the viewer and the history in the main column,
    the file's facts beside it — the facts, its projects, its connections and its notes, outward-in
    as the page always read them. pages/preview.css lays the two columns out from 1280px and stacks
    them below that. The blocks inside are MOVED here, not edited; the page head and the
    move-conflict prompt stay above.

    A LASER OR CNC FILE'S RECIPE PANEL HEADS THE SIDE COLUMN (2026-10-25, at the user's request). It
    sat beside the preview inside the main column, each taking half, which left the Measure view a
    narrow square beside a table mostly made of whitespace. The viewer now has the whole main column
    and the recipe sits above the facts. `has-recipe` widens the side column for these pages only,
    because a five-column layer table does not fit the 340px every other file page keeps. %>
<% recipe = @thumbnail&.metadata.presence %>
<div class="workspace<%= " has-recipe" if recipe %>">
<div class="workspace-main">
<%# THE PAGE FINISHES ITS OWN PREVIEW (stage 4, S4-3). One `thumbs` controller on the row covers
    every branch below — the Measure poster, the 3D poster, the Gerber poster and the plain
    thumbnail all show the same server render, so one poll answers for all four instead of four
    copies of a marker. It costs a connected controller and ZERO requests on a page with nothing
    pending: `pendingIds()` is empty, so `poll()` returns before it fetches.

    RELOAD MODE, NOT AN IMAGE SWAP, AND THE REASON IS THAT THE WHOLE PAGE IS STALE. The four
    posters have four different resting markups (a poster plus a download link, a status paragraph
    a Gerber controller targets, a bare sentence), so swapping an image in would put the JS in
    charge of markup the ERB owns, in four shapes. And the picture is not the only thing waiting on
    that render: the Laser Settings and CNC panels are drawn from @thumbnail.metadata, and Size and
    Modified come from the same stat. A reload is one mechanism and the honest one. %>
<div class="preview-row"
     data-controller="thumbs" data-thumbs-url-value="<%= thumbnails_files_path %>"
     data-thumbs-reload-value="true">
  <%# THE POLL SET, DECLARED RATHER THAN INFERRED. Grid cells announce themselves as pending by
      holding a `.thumb-spinner`; there is no spinner here, so this element says so outright and
      the controller clears the flag the moment an answer arrives. Rendered ONLY while a render is
      genuinely in flight AND there is nothing to show yet: a name with no renderer has no row and
      nothing coming, a ready row has arrived, and a rebuilding row is already showing its picture
      of this content. That last one must not poll — /files/thumbnails answers ready for a row with
      a picture, so the reload would render the same rebuilding row and poll again, in a loop. %>
  <% if @thumbnail&.in_flight? && !@thumbnail.showable? %>
    <span class="preview-poll" hidden data-thumbs-target="cell"
          data-node-id="<%= @node.node_id %>" data-thumbs-pending="true"></span>
  <% end %>
  <div class="preview">
  <%# THE BANNER IS NOT PART OF THE VIEWER and stays on this page: it is a fact about a
      REPOSITORY and about who is looking at it (whether this box can commit, how many edits are
      pending), neither of which a visitor's page or the model pane has or should have. Rendered
      where the chain's :text branch used to render it, right before the code viewer. %>
  <% if @info.content_token && @kind == :text && @repo_root %>
    <div class="repo-banner">
      <% if @committable_here %>
        <%= link_to repo_commit_path(@repo_root.node_id), class: "repo-banner-link" do %>
          <%= @pending_count.positive? ? pluralize(@pending_count, "pending change") : "No pending changes" %>
          in <%= @repo_root.name %> — review &amp; commit
        <% end %>
      <% else %>
        <span class="muted">Saved changes sync to the machine that holds
        <%= @repo_root.name %>, where you can commit them with git.</span>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
  <%= render "files/viewer", viewer: @viewer, editable: @editable, save: true %>
  </div>
</div>

<%# THE HISTORY PANEL, FOR ANY FILE INSIDE A REPOSITORY THIS BOX CAN READ -- not only a text one.
    Its rows arrive by fetch AFTER this page renders (history_controller.js): a cold capped walk is
    ~104 ms and a full one measured 4,917 ms over the live CIFS mount, and the file page must not
    wait on either (tinkerex#178, spec part 3 rule 2). So there is nothing to server-render here
    beyond the mount and the URLs it needs.

    GATED ON @committable_here, WHICH IS THE CAPABILITY AND NOT A SECOND REPOSITORY CHECK. It reads
    "this repository is on a local filesystem this server can open", which is the same precondition
    reading history has -- FilesController#history answers "no_worktree" without it, and the
    repository-wide page redirects. One question, one answer (§14a); the banner above already says
    what happens to a save on a deployment that has no working tree.

    data-controller IS A LITERAL STRING AND MUST STAY ONE. test/stimulus_hygiene_test.rb scans the
    literal attribute, so an interpolated name resolves to nothing it can check -- on the one
    attribute whose failure mode is a SILENT no-op with no console warning (§14d, nine days once).
    Never write a percent followed by a greater-than inside one of these comments either: an ERB
    comment ends at the first one and the rest of this prose prints into the page.

    THE OID IN THE TWO TEMPLATED URLS IS A HEX PLACEHOLDER, NOT A WORD. Both routes constrain :oid
    to hex, and a route constraint binds URL GENERATION as well as recognition, so a readable
    placeholder raises ActionController::UrlGenerationError at render time. The controller
    substitutes the real oid. %>
<% if @repo_root && @committable_here %>
  <section class="history-panel"
           data-controller="history"
           data-history-url-value="<%= history_file_path(@node.node_id) %>"
           data-history-blob-url-value="<%= history_blob_file_path(@node.node_id, oid: "0" * 40) %>"
           data-history-restore-url-value="<%= restore_file_path(@node.node_id, oid: "0" * 40) %>"
           data-history-work-url-value="<%= blob_file_path(@node.node_id) %>">
    <h2>History
      <%= link_to "All history in #{@repo_root.name}", repo_history_path(@repo_root.node_id),
                  class: "muted history-repo-link" %>
    </h2>
    <div class="history-rows" data-history-target="rows">
      <p class="muted">Loading history…</p>
    </div>
  </section>
<% end %>
</div>

<aside class="workspace-side" aria-label="About this file">
<%# Which recipe panel: MillMage writes "operations" (an endmill, feeds, depths), LightBurn
    writes "layers" (speed/power/passes). Branch on the key rather than on the file extension —
    the metadata is what the panel actually renders, and a .mage that parsed no operations has
    nothing for the CNC panel to show either way. FIRST in the column, above the facts: on a laser
    or CNC file it is what the page is opened for. %>
<% if recipe %>
  <% if recipe["operations"].present? %>
    <%= render "files/mage_settings", meta: recipe %>
  <% else %>
    <%= render "files/lbrn_settings", meta: recipe %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
<%= render "files/meta", info: @info, addon_origin: addon_origin, library_path: @library_path %>
<%# RENAME OR MOVE, under the facts it changes rather than in the page head: an open composer there
    would stretch the head's row of buttons. FILES ONLY, and not inside a git working tree (the same
    @repo_root the banner and History read): exactly what FilesController#rename and #move accept,
    since a folder reached by its URL renders this page too (FilesController::FOLDERS_NOT_YET). %>
<% if @node.file? && @repo_root.nil? %>
  <%= render "files/rename_move", node: @node, library_path: @library_path, return_to: "file" %>
<% end %>
<%= render "files/attach", node: @node, attached_projects: @attached_projects, projects: @projects,
                           return_to: "file" %>

<%# THE CONNECTED PANEL (stage 4, S4-8), immediately before the notes: a file's records read
    outward-in — what it is tied to, then what you wrote about it. %>
<%= render "files/connections" %>

<%# id="notes" is the inspector's door in: its note count and "Add a note" link here as #notes
    (files/inspector.html.erb), because the composer is a Lexxy editor and belongs on this page. %>
<section class="notes-panel" id="notes">
  <h2>Notes <span class="muted">(<%= @notes.size %>)</span></h2>

  <%# CLOSED UNTIL ASKED FOR (spec §8): the editor's whole toolbar used to sit open on every file
      page with nothing to write. The form, its scope, its field and the Lexxy element are
      unchanged; only a <details> wraps them. Lexxy mounts inside a closed details and lays itself
      out when it opens — verified by test/system/files_pages_test.rb, which types a note through
      it. Keep the ERB comment above the form that explains `scope: :note`. %>
  <details class="composer">
    <summary class="btn small">Add a note</summary>
    <%# scope: :note — Maker::Note is namespaced, so form_with would otherwise derive
        `maker_note[...]` and NotesController's params.dig(:note, :body) would read nil. %>
    <%= form_with model: @new_note, scope: :note, url: file_notes_path(@node.node_id),
                  method: :post, class: "note-form" do |f| %>
      <%= f.rich_text_area :body, placeholder: "Add a note…", attachments: "false" %>
      <%= f.submit "Add note", class: "btn small" %>
    <% end %>
  </details>

  <% if @notes.empty? %>
    <p class="muted">No notes yet.</p>
  <% else %>
    <% @notes.each do |note| %>
      <article class="note">
        <div class="note-body"><%= note.body %></div>
        <div class="note-meta muted">
          <time><%= note.created_at.strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M") %></time>
          <%= button_to "Delete", note_path(note), method: :delete, class: "linkbtn danger small",
                        form: { data: { turbo_confirm: "Delete this note?" } } %>
        </div>
      </article>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</section>
</aside>
</div>
